1. Definition and Core MechanismButtazzo defines the Polling Server (PS) as a functional periodic task$(T_s, C_s)$dedicated to serving incoming asynchronous, aperiodic requests.The Activation Rule: The server is scheduled at a fixed priority alongside other periodic tasks (typically using Rate Monotonic priority assignment).The Execution Rule: When the Polling Server is activated, it handles any pending aperiodic requests in its queue up to its maximum capacity.$C_s$. If the requests are completed or the budget reaches zero, the server is suspended until the start of its next macro-period.Reference: Buttazzo, GC Hard Real-Time Computing Systems: Predictable Scheduling Algorithms and Applications . Springer. (Chapter 5: "Fixed-Priority Servers").2. The "Capacity Waste" PhenomenonThe most critical theoretical insight highlighted by Buttazzo is that the Polling Server does not preserve its execution budget if no requests are present upon its release.Mechanism of Failure: If the Polling Server is activated by the scheduler and finds the aperiodic queue completely empty, it immediately discharges its budget, setting its capacity to zero ($C_s = 0$) , and suspends itself.The Consequence: If an aperiodic request arrives even a fraction of a microsecond after the server has discharged its capacity, it cannot be handled immediately. It must wait in the queue until the next period ($T_s$) begins and the budget is refreshed.Reference: Buttazzo, GC Hard Real-Time Computing Systems . Springer. Chapter 5, Section on Polling Server.3. Worst-Case Response Time FormulaBecause of the misalignment penalty caused by capacity wasting, Buttazzo establishes the mathematical upper bounds for aperiodic tasks handled by a Polling Server.For an isolated aperiodic task with a computation time$C_a \le C_s$, the worst-case response time$R_{max}$occurs when the task arrives immediately after the server has checked an empty queue and gone to sleep. The task must sit through the remainder of the current period, plus the server's execution duration in the next period:$$R_{max} = T_s + C_s$$If the aperiodic task is large and requires multiple periods of execution ($C_a > C_s$), the worst-case response time scales step-wise:$$R_{max} = \left\lceil \frac{C_a}{C_s} \right\rceil T_s + C_s$$Reference: Buttazzo, GC Hard Real-Time Computing Systems . Springer. Chapter 5 ("Polling Server Performance Evaluation").4. Schedulability and Sizing AnalysisOne of the primary benefits of the Polling Server highlighted by Buttazzo is its perfect compatibility with classical scheduling tests.Utilization Bound: Because a Polling Server behaves exactly like a standard periodic task, a hybrid system containing$n$hard periodic tasks and one Polling Server can be verified using the classic Liu and Layland bound under Rate Monotonic scheduling:$$U = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \frac{C_i}{T_i} + \frac{C_s}{T_s} \le (n+1)\left(2^{\frac{1}{n+1}} - 1\right)$$Design trade-off: Buttazzo notes that reducing$T_s$improves the average-case response time of aperiodic requests. However, as$T_s$decreases, the server's utilization footprint ($U_s = C_s / T_s$) climbs rapidly, making it more difficult to guarantee that the hard periodic tasks will meet their deadlines.Reference: Buttazzo, GC Hard Real-Time Computing Systems . Springer. Chapter 4 & 5 (Rate Monotonic Hybrid Schedulability).5. Comparative Evaluation (Alternatives)To address the limitations of the Polling Server, Buttazzo evaluates the transition to bandwidth-preserving servers :Deferrable Server (DS): Unlike the PS, the Deferrable Server preserves its budget if no aperiodic tasks are ready when it wakes up. The budget stays available throughout the entire period. While this lowers aperiodic latency, Buttazzo points out that it degrades the worst-case schedulability bound for lower-priority periodic tasks due to "back-to-back" server execution.Sporadic Server (SS): The Sporadic Server preserves its capacity but replenishes it dynamically based on when tasks actually execute, rather than on a rigid periodic schedule. Buttazzo indicates that the Sporadic Server solves the utilization penalty of the DS but requires a much higher kernel footprint to manage dynamic timers.Reference: Buttazzo, GC Hard Real-Time Computing Systems . Springer. Chapter 5 ("Comparative Evaluation of Fixed-Priority Servers").
